{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"18feef79-be28-48c1-86ab-df3c81730cd4","status":"SUCCESS","response":"I have fixed the generator in [gen.py](file:///tmp/h-DNqpXE/gen.py) and documented the changes and rationale in [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-DNqpXE/ANSWER.md).\n\n### Summary of Changes\n\n1. **[gen.py](file:///tmp/h-DNqpXE/gen.py#L10-L13)**: In [`hr_target()`](file:///tmp/h-DNqpXE/gen.py#L10-L13), changed the lower bound dictionary key from `\"value\"` to `\"start\"`:\n   ```python\n   def hr_target(step):\n       \"\"\"HR corridor for a step, as a percentage of the athlete's LTHR.\"\"\"\n       lo, hi = step[\"hr_pct\"]\n       return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n   ```\n\n2. **Root Cause**:\n   - In Intervals.icu's `workout_doc` schema, a target range/corridor requires `\"start\"` and `\"end\"` keys, whereas `\"value\"` designates a single target value.\n   - When `\"value\": lo` was used alongside `\"end\": hi`, Intervals.icu ignored `\"end\"` and treated the step as a single point intensity target (`value: lo`).\n   - When exported to Garmin Connect, Intervals.icu mapped the single target percentage to the athlete's corresponding discrete HR zone (e.g., 68% $\\to$ Zone 2, 88% $\\to$ Zone 4).\n   - The Garmin watch received zone-based targets (`HEART_RATE_ZONE`) and clamped them to its local zone definitions.\n   - Using `\"start\": lo, \"end\": hi` ensures Intervals.icu treats the step as an explicit corridor, resolves it against the athlete's LTHR (172 bpm) into exact BPM bounds, and exports custom target boundaries instead of device zone numbers.\n","duration_seconds":447.293764,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":202759,"output_tokens":24707,"thinking_tokens":21989,"cache_read_tokens":2027341,"total_tokens":227466}}}
